WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.807
In this example, we define a Product class.

00:00:02.807 --> 00:00:06.062
The __init__ method stores the product name and price.

00:00:06.062 --> 00:00:09.422
Then we define a method called sale_price.

00:00:09.422 --> 00:00:15.652
This method calculates a discounted price using the product's own price.

00:00:15.652 --> 00:00:17.777
We create two objects: item1 and item2.

00:00:17.777 --> 00:00:21.427
Item1 is a laptop with a price of 900.

00:00:21.427 --> 00:00:25.037
Item2 is a monitor with a price of 300.

00:00:25.037 --> 00:00:28.892
Then we print each item's regular price and sale price.

00:00:28.892 --> 00:00:32.907
Notice that both objects use the same sale_price method.

00:00:32.907 --> 00:00:38.232
But the method uses the price stored inside the specific object.

00:00:38.232 --> 00:00:40.522
For item1, self.price is 900.

00:00:40.522 --> 00:00:44.167
For item2, self.price is 300.

00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:46.262
This is why self matters.

00:00:46.262 --> 00:00:52.177
It allows the method to work with the data stored in the object that called it.

00:00:52.177 --> 00:00:57.432
After running the code, try changing the discount rate from 0.10 to 0.20.

00:00:57.432 --> 00:01:00.067
Then compare how the sale prices change.
